00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:55.960
mentioned with the NFL.

00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:58.000
Data platforms.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:01.090
We have introduced a data
platform that aggregates all

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:04.270
that data-- it's powered by
Google-- called Savannah--

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:06.970
aggregates all of
that IoT data so

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:09.400
that we can create solutions--

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:10.570
software and solutions.

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:14.200
And our partners can also
create those solutions.

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:17.500
Services and supplies
round out all of those ways

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:21.410
that we're solving
problems for our customers.

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:23.380
And the way that
we think about it

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:28.920
is we sense what's going
on out on that edge,

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:30.960
analyze it, and act on it.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:32.610
That is Zebra Technologies.

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:35.980
We're not that printer
company that we grew up to be,

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:39.470
we're definitely that
end-to-end solution company.

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:39.970
All right.

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:42.750
Let's go back to printers.

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:48.210
I've been staring at
this slide for a while.

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:51.360
And I am so proud
when I look at this.

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:53.160
I think printers
are a work of art.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:58.410
These mission critical thermal
transfer-- thermal printers

00:09:58.410 --> 00:09:59.940
are really a work of art.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:02.200
And when you look at
this picture here,

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:05.490
we've built out this
powerful OS that

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:08.640
takes care of that real time
component, like I mentioned,

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:12.490
and on top of it, we've built
all of these applications.

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:14.970
And at the end of the
day you look at it--

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:19.170
we're solving-- we're building
this horizontal solution

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:23.760
set, this ecosystem, in order
to solve problems and help

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:26.440
our customers in four key
verticals-- in retail,

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:31.050
health care, T&amp;O,
and in manufacturing.

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:35.830
You look at this and say we
are solving problems every day,

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:36.785
but--

00:10:36.785 --> 00:10:40.150
and I don't say
but, but-- and there

00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:44.140
are still unique needs out
there that our customers demand.

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:45.650
There are new use cases.

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:48.795
There are new application needs.

00:10:48.795 --> 00:10:49.670
So what have we done?

00:10:49.670 --> 00:10:51.500
We created a custom
application group--

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:53.380
all of this internally--

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:56.410
to build and solve
those customer issues.

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:59.410
But we-- and we solve
those problems every day--

00:10:59.410 --> 00:11:04.500
from standalone
application, emulations,

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:08.520
integrations-- all of them with
that custom application group.

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:14.550
They solve hundreds of customer
unique cases every year.

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:19.120
But think if where there was
a way that we could open up

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:22.240
this powerful engine that we
have here, that we've built,

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:24.010
and make it
available to others--

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:27.220
make it available to
outside developers

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:30.670
in order to go faster and allow
them to solve those problems.

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:35.380
So that's where this unique
solution comes in with MicroEJ.

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:39.910
So take a look at what this
looks like and imagine building

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:41.090
a secure--

00:11:41.090 --> 00:11:42.590
and that's the most
important part--

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:47.050
a secure Java-based application
engine that sits on top of all

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:51.340
of that real time stuff
that Zebra will take care

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:54.100
of, so that you can go faster--

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:58.570
taking advantage of all of
those standard libraries that

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:00.880
exist in Java--

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:04.750
allows you to go faster,
solve unique problems more

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:08.650
efficiently, and keep
that real time stuff

00:12:08.650 --> 00:12:12.070
and keep that application
stuff separated.

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:13.900
If you step back and
look at it, it really

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:18.220
looks like that app
revolution that you

00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:24.890
saw in the mobile world coming
into the embedded printing

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:25.700
world.

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:28.160
It's a very powerful
picture where

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:31.650
we're enabling external
developers really

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:33.300
three benefits.

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:36.990
We enable external
developers to develop

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:41.290
these new and unique use cases
and we see them every day,

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:45.505
maybe web browsing, template
printing that is required

00:12:45.505 --> 00:12:46.755
that you can develop yourself.

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:53.140
Second, it probably and it will
help our internal development.

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:56.080
I mentioned all that work
that goes on internally,

00:12:56.080 --> 00:12:59.470
now we can start to take
advantage of the same Java

00:12:59.470 --> 00:13:02.980
capabilities, and build
out those capabilities,

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:06.050
and some of them end up
in that channel release

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:09.040
that I showed you on
the previous slide.

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:12.190
And third-- there is a third
subtle one that you see here.

00:13:12.190 --> 00:13:14.830
You've got this powerful engine
that's sitting on the edge--

00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:20.910
performing on the edge, let's
give it more responsibility,

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:24.048
take on some of the
hardware that's out there,

00:13:24.048 --> 00:13:26.340
and get rid of that hardware,
and let the printer do it

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:27.450
itself--

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:28.890
write those
applications yourself.

00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:33.910
So where are we at with this?

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:37.470
So recently-- a few months ago
at the Consumer Electronics

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:38.310
Show--

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:43.850
we showed two demos that show
you the possibilities here.

00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:48.110
First, we implemented and
connected to the Google IoT

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:52.730
platform, implemented MQTT
very quickly through that Java

00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:57.180
engine, took advantage
of the Twitter client,

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:00.000
and were able to send messages
back and forth very quickly

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:05.710
to a card printer and
a thermal printer.

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:09.370
And, third, we were able to take
advantage of the Google Maps

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:11.920
client, which we'll
see a demo on,

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:17.260
to be able to show location
information directly on a card

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:19.360
and on the UI--

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:24.880
all of this coming from that
Java-based application engine.

00:14:24.880 --> 00:14:27.820
So you can think of
the possibilities

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:31.480
of keeping that
application layer separated

00:14:31.480 --> 00:14:33.220
from that real time layer--

