00:21:18.050 --> 00:21:24.230
And it's done for any
hardware, any of those--

00:21:24.230 --> 00:21:27.230
let's say, [INAUDIBLE]
OS underneath.

00:21:27.230 --> 00:21:30.890
The behavior of that piece
of code, that software,

00:21:30.890 --> 00:21:31.860
remains the same.

00:21:31.860 --> 00:21:36.320
So you don't have to retest,
recompile, rebuild, et cetera.

00:21:36.320 --> 00:21:40.790
This is one-- you write
this software agent once

00:21:40.790 --> 00:21:46.350
and it can run and be deployed
on any device, any printer.

00:21:46.350 --> 00:21:47.770
So how does it work?

00:21:47.770 --> 00:21:49.610
So I've been asked
to give you a bit

00:21:49.610 --> 00:21:52.320
of a sense of the technology.

00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:56.210
So, obviously, if
you look at what

00:21:56.210 --> 00:22:02.630
has been done on the Android,
for example, when Android came

00:22:02.630 --> 00:22:08.480
up, we were able to
phone and, let's say,

00:22:08.480 --> 00:22:16.190
as soon Android came up five
years later, 80% of the phone

00:22:16.190 --> 00:22:21.410
were running Android and
that marketplace for the apps

00:22:21.410 --> 00:22:23.540
were roughly one million apps.

00:22:23.540 --> 00:22:30.200
So it mean that if you are able
to let people write application

00:22:30.200 --> 00:22:31.910
on any hardware--

00:22:31.910 --> 00:22:36.650
because all these
smartphone are very diverse,

00:22:36.650 --> 00:22:38.340
various hardware, et cetera--

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:40.370
it means that you can
create a lot of value

00:22:40.370 --> 00:22:44.730
out of this concept
of virtualization.

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:47.750
So this is exactly what
we've done at MicroEJ.

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:53.240
We've been able to create
a small, tiny [? sibling ?]

00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:56.360
of virtualization that
is used by Android

00:22:56.360 --> 00:23:01.580
and we target all device
that are cost effective.

00:23:01.580 --> 00:23:05.390
Because, in fact, we
had to reduce the size

00:23:05.390 --> 00:23:08.960
of the core engine of MicroEJ--

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:12.210
of Android by 1,000
times smaller.

00:23:12.210 --> 00:23:18.710
So, basically, we
target every processors

00:23:18.710 --> 00:23:23.210
that are below $12 to $15.

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:26.300
And the size of
the engine itself

00:23:26.300 --> 00:23:29.960
is roughly 30k which is
very, very, very small.

00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:32.840
We can even say tiny.

00:23:32.840 --> 00:23:37.040
So let's go a bit--

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:40.550
let's go through what we do.

00:23:40.550 --> 00:23:45.230
Most people tend to compare
[? earth ?] to Android,

00:23:45.230 --> 00:23:49.560
so that's how we
position ourselves.

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:57.510
And in terms of, I would
say, what makes that happen--

00:23:57.510 --> 00:24:01.430
so one key message is
that virtualization

00:24:01.430 --> 00:24:04.790
is something that, in
fact, allow you to decouple

00:24:04.790 --> 00:24:06.960
the hardware from the software.

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:11.570
And so this is,
basically, what has

00:24:11.570 --> 00:24:14.965
been done-- reducing
the size of the needed,

00:24:14.965 --> 00:24:20.310
let's say, layer to
virtualize a software.

00:24:20.310 --> 00:24:24.930
So, yeah, we have some kind of
companion, Android on one side

00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:29.610
and MicroEJ on the other side,
to target all these devices.

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:33.960
And something which is
very important for IoT--

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:37.478
creating a device, connecting
the device to the cloud--

00:24:37.478 --> 00:24:39.270
that's quite easy, in
fact, and [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:39.270 --> 00:24:42.390
something well-known,
well-addressed by lots of,

00:24:42.390 --> 00:24:44.220
let's say, industry.

00:24:44.220 --> 00:24:46.580
But as soon you have
a fleet of device--

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:48.970
lots of different,
various devices--

00:24:48.970 --> 00:24:52.920
they are not at all the
same in terms of hardware,

00:24:52.920 --> 00:24:55.960
in terms of software
running on it.

00:24:55.960 --> 00:24:59.280
The OS is different,
the version of the OS,

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:03.190
all that change along
the time and there

00:25:03.190 --> 00:25:08.670
is a need to be
able to, let's say,

00:25:08.670 --> 00:25:12.640
capture and create software
assets on top of that.

00:25:12.640 --> 00:25:15.360
So this is a virtualization
that, in fact, we've

00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:20.090
been able to downsize from
30 mega to, let's say,

00:25:20.090 --> 00:25:26.040
30k in terms of size
of the footprint--

00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:28.300
of the virtualization layer.

00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:32.310
So if you think
as someone that is

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:36.030
in need to design
a new device, you

00:25:36.030 --> 00:25:39.180
will have to find
the right processor.

00:25:39.180 --> 00:25:44.010
You have right there,
roughly, 1,000--

00:25:44.010 --> 00:25:46.380
or 10,000 different processors.

00:25:46.380 --> 00:25:50.440
Compilers, you have, roughly,
four to five compilers.

00:25:50.440 --> 00:25:53.640
OSes, same, less
than 10, et cetera.

00:25:53.640 --> 00:25:56.910
If you multiply all that,
the combination of all that

00:25:56.910 --> 00:26:03.330
is what we call the metrics
of pain for the software.

00:26:03.330 --> 00:26:08.870
And it's-- this huge diversity
is almost impossible to handle

00:26:08.870 --> 00:26:11.110
in a very cost effective way.

00:26:11.110 --> 00:26:14.280
So let's think about
Android when they came up.

00:26:14.280 --> 00:26:18.480
Just after four or
five years, they

00:26:18.480 --> 00:26:20.970
took all the market
of the smartphone

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:23.550
just because, in fact, when
you have an application,

00:26:23.550 --> 00:26:26.800
you can download
it on any device.

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:30.630
And the next device
we'll be able to load

00:26:30.630 --> 00:26:32.470
the old application, et cetera.

00:26:32.470 --> 00:26:35.930
So it means that you take out
whole lifecycle of the software

00:26:35.930 --> 00:26:38.100
asset from the hardware.

00:26:41.640 --> 00:26:44.430
So we are pretty much similar.

00:26:44.430 --> 00:26:47.550
We provide virtual
device, multi-sandbox,

00:26:47.550 --> 00:26:50.520
application store,
the two main languages

00:26:50.520 --> 00:26:52.757
are C and Java for that program.

00:26:52.757 --> 00:26:54.340
Of course, we support
other languages,

00:26:54.340 --> 00:26:56.850
but that's the
main two languages.

00:26:56.850 --> 00:27:01.350
In terms of markets, we
target probably the same kind

00:27:01.350 --> 00:27:04.390
of market, but only on the
cost effective devices--

00:27:04.390 --> 00:27:12.340
so wearables, medical devices,
energy meters, medical device,

00:27:12.340 --> 00:27:16.200
lightning [INAUDIBLE],,
obviously, building automation,

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:17.730
smart home, et cetera.

00:27:17.730 --> 00:27:22.230
We roughly have 30 million
devices followed by MicroEJ

00:27:22.230 --> 00:27:26.890
that has been sold so far now.

00:27:26.890 --> 00:27:31.750
And we partnered with Google
to provide a very seamless way

00:27:31.750 --> 00:27:35.900
to connect and to have,
let's say, the plumbing--

00:27:35.900 --> 00:27:38.920
the plumbing between the devices
and the cloud is something

00:27:38.920 --> 00:27:39.460
that's--

00:27:39.460 --> 00:27:43.700
shall not be, let's say,
handled by the developers,

00:27:43.700 --> 00:27:46.450
especially if the-- just
think about an evolution

00:27:46.450 --> 00:27:48.070
of the plumbing.

00:27:48.070 --> 00:27:50.740
Of course, there will
be a V2, V3, V4--

00:27:50.740 --> 00:27:52.930
and what about all
the application

00:27:52.930 --> 00:27:54.330
running on both sides?

00:27:54.330 --> 00:27:57.648
So the plumbing needs to be able
to evolve without impacting,

00:27:57.648 --> 00:28:00.190
in fact, the applications-- the
assets that have been created

00:28:00.190 --> 00:28:01.810
by your ecosystems.

00:28:01.810 --> 00:28:04.960
So that's what we do
with Google as partner.

00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:08.292
We provide the
edge computing side

00:28:08.292 --> 00:28:10.000
in terms of the
virtualization and Google

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:14.330
provides the cloud part.

00:28:14.330 --> 00:28:16.962
So Victor mentioned something
very, very important--

00:28:16.962 --> 00:28:19.930
security--

00:28:19.930 --> 00:28:23.900
security for a lot
of various devices.

00:28:23.900 --> 00:28:28.300
So how do we do and do we
ensure that there is no way

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:32.800
for an application to go
into the printer engine

00:28:32.800 --> 00:28:36.730
and that's-- you can, in
fact, open your platform--

00:28:36.730 --> 00:28:41.810
the printers to an ecosystem of
independent software vendors,

00:28:41.810 --> 00:28:45.530
so for them to create
new apps, et cetera.

00:28:45.530 --> 00:28:48.880
So it needs to be
very bulletproof

00:28:48.880 --> 00:28:52.580
and there is no
[? compromise ?] on security.

00:28:52.580 --> 00:28:55.540
So we do that with sandboxes.

00:28:55.540 --> 00:29:01.270
So we use sandbox, so each
means that every application is,

00:29:01.270 --> 00:29:06.700
in fact, into its own
container and it cannot escape.

00:29:06.700 --> 00:29:13.030
And the one way to
do that is to reuse

00:29:13.030 --> 00:29:16.540
what has been used for
smartcard, in fact.

00:29:16.540 --> 00:29:19.600
So we tend to use the very
kind of technology that

00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:21.340
are, in fact, for smartcard.

00:29:21.340 --> 00:29:23.710
So you can load an
application into a smartcard,

00:29:23.710 --> 00:29:27.800
same for here, but the
application can't get out.

00:29:27.800 --> 00:29:31.780
And so the container,
the sandbox,

00:29:31.780 --> 00:29:36.250
controls the execution of
the application, the grant--

00:29:36.250 --> 00:29:39.880
if he wants-- if it
wants to communicate

00:29:39.880 --> 00:29:43.120
underneath with a Zebra
co-printing activities.

00:29:43.120 --> 00:29:46.962
So all that is completely
under control of the trusted

00:29:46.962 --> 00:29:47.920
execution environment--

00:29:47.920 --> 00:29:50.110
MicroEJ trusted
execution environment.

00:29:50.110 --> 00:29:52.730
We call that V for virtual
execution environment.

00:29:52.730 --> 00:29:57.980
And so if V is
execution environment

00:29:57.980 --> 00:30:02.020
allow the printer to
connect to the Google Cloud,

00:30:02.020 --> 00:30:05.080
then the application will be
able-- all the application will

00:30:05.080 --> 00:30:08.950
be able to talk to
the Google agent

00:30:08.950 --> 00:30:13.570
and then be able to publish
that asset to the cloud

00:30:13.570 --> 00:30:15.400
to get analytics.

00:30:18.130 --> 00:30:21.710
So all that is something
that is well-known from the,

00:30:21.710 --> 00:30:25.090
let's say, technical
point of view,

00:30:25.090 --> 00:30:29.590
but how do you ensure that the
sandbox itself is protected,

00:30:29.590 --> 00:30:32.140
is well-designed,
and there is no flaw,

00:30:32.140 --> 00:30:35.590
and you can tweak it so
that you can, in fact, get

00:30:35.590 --> 00:30:40.350
into the underneath
labor, the core printing

00:30:40.350 --> 00:30:42.700
that you want to protect.

00:30:42.700 --> 00:30:45.250
So that's my technical
slide, in fact.

00:30:45.250 --> 00:30:52.000
So we have what we call
binary code inspection.

00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:58.210
So we are able to verify before
the application is loaded,

00:30:58.210 --> 00:31:03.670
or complied on, or
linked to your device,

00:31:03.670 --> 00:31:07.310
that it does not have
any malicious code.

00:31:07.310 --> 00:31:11.410
So we can do that by
doing a huge analysis

00:31:11.410 --> 00:31:14.620
of the binary code,
your software,

00:31:14.620 --> 00:31:19.240
and if it doesn't fit the
semantics of the sandbox,

00:31:19.240 --> 00:31:21.340
of the virtual
execution environment,

00:31:21.340 --> 00:31:23.260
then it is not allowed to run.

00:31:23.260 --> 00:31:26.430
And doing-- thanks
to that, there

00:31:26.430 --> 00:31:33.290
is no way to overcome the
trusted execution environment.

00:31:33.290 --> 00:31:39.940
And that has been proved in
the let's say, 20 years ago

00:31:39.940 --> 00:31:41.020
for the smartcard.

00:31:41.020 --> 00:31:42.220
So there is no way to--

00:31:42.220 --> 00:31:44.560
from the, let's
say, the application

00:31:44.560 --> 00:31:48.400
itself to attack the
underneath system.

00:31:48.400 --> 00:31:52.150
So think about the system
where the mass majority

00:31:52.150 --> 00:31:55.930
of application are, in fact,
controlled by this trusted

00:31:55.930 --> 00:31:57.210
execution environment.

00:31:57.210 --> 00:32:00.070
It means that this--

00:32:00.070 --> 00:32:02.290
the attackers, the
people that wants to,

00:32:02.290 --> 00:32:05.550
in fact, take into
the controls of device

00:32:05.550 --> 00:32:08.130
to do bad things with it--

00:32:08.130 --> 00:32:09.670
the size of the
code that you need

00:32:09.670 --> 00:32:12.970
to be sure that there is no
burden, no flow, et cetera,

00:32:12.970 --> 00:32:16.010
is reduced to the
minimum [INAUDIBLE]..

00:32:16.010 --> 00:32:19.660
And so that's helps a lot
in terms of having a cost

00:32:19.660 --> 00:32:21.070
effective device.

00:32:21.070 --> 00:32:25.090
It means that you protect
yourself from the attackers,

00:32:25.090 --> 00:32:31.320
you open the device to a large
majority of a large community

00:32:31.320 --> 00:32:34.358
and still you protect
your core engine.

00:32:34.358 --> 00:32:35.150
That's the purpose.

00:32:35.150 --> 00:32:40.590
So virtualization
around MicroEJ has been

00:32:40.590 --> 00:32:42.560
going through certification--

00:32:42.560 --> 00:32:44.010
[INAUDIBLE] certification.

00:32:44.010 --> 00:32:46.350
For those of you
that know what it is,

00:32:46.350 --> 00:32:49.320
that means there
is a probability

00:32:49.320 --> 00:32:51.770
of bug very, very, very low--

00:32:51.770 --> 00:32:54.210
almost none.

00:32:54.210 --> 00:32:59.040
So combined with
the binary checking,

00:32:59.040 --> 00:33:00.810
it's a very robust solution.

00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:08.850
So this is almost my last slide.

00:33:08.850 --> 00:33:15.270
So what we've been able
to create with our partner

00:33:15.270 --> 00:33:18.040
Google for Zebra.

00:33:18.040 --> 00:33:23.460
So there's Zebra engineers--
they create printers,

00:33:23.460 --> 00:33:26.790
they know very well
their job, how to print.

00:33:26.790 --> 00:33:30.540
That's something very hard,
as Victor's explained,

00:33:30.540 --> 00:33:35.940
it's a real time engine, timing
needs to be very accurate.

00:33:35.940 --> 00:33:42.120
And so in order to-- so that's
their core capabilities that

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:46.040
needs to be protected, so
it provide this print--

00:33:46.040 --> 00:33:49.230
these printers with
a printing engine.

00:33:49.230 --> 00:33:52.230
On top of that, you want
to have a community that

00:33:52.230 --> 00:33:55.380
creates applications
like a Twitter,

00:33:55.380 --> 00:34:03.120
being able to load a
map, being able to create

00:34:03.120 --> 00:34:07.440
a fancy card by adjusting
maps, addresses, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:34:07.440 --> 00:34:08.969
et cetera, on their printer.

00:34:08.969 --> 00:34:10.679
All that is quite easy to do.

00:34:10.679 --> 00:34:14.860
A regular engineer can
do that in a few days.

00:34:14.860 --> 00:34:17.830
So in order to give them
the possibility to do that,

00:34:17.830 --> 00:34:19.449
you create a virtual device.

00:34:19.449 --> 00:34:23.610
And then you can create
your app on your PC

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:27.929
without even having a
printer on your desk.

00:34:27.929 --> 00:34:30.150
Obviously, having all
the printers on your desk

00:34:30.150 --> 00:34:32.440
is quite a challenge.

00:34:32.440 --> 00:34:33.690
So that's the right--

00:34:33.690 --> 00:34:36.030
the left-- the right part.

00:34:36.030 --> 00:34:38.340
So you have what we call
an [INAUDIBLE] [? repair ?]

00:34:38.340 --> 00:34:41.370
studio, so that you can create
apps and then, of course,

00:34:41.370 --> 00:34:45.420
benefit from the
ecosystem with a store

00:34:45.420 --> 00:34:49.900
where you can load and store
all the software assets.

00:34:49.900 --> 00:34:53.639
Then, underneath, you have this
trusted execution [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:53.639 --> 00:34:55.929
where you load all these apps--

00:34:55.929 --> 00:34:58.360
that you can download
them dynamically,

00:34:58.360 --> 00:35:01.210
meaning that you have a
new protocol coming in,

00:35:01.210 --> 00:35:04.540
you have your device
a printer in the field

00:35:04.540 --> 00:35:08.500
since, let's say, five years,
and you have a new protocol,

00:35:08.500 --> 00:35:11.920
then you can then load this
new protocol into its sandbox

00:35:11.920 --> 00:35:13.850
and still it's going to be safe.

00:35:13.850 --> 00:35:17.770
You don't need to reassess
all the other printer.

00:35:17.770 --> 00:35:20.110
And, of course,
there's these agents

00:35:20.110 --> 00:35:24.340
on the right that allow you
to communicate with the Google

00:35:24.340 --> 00:35:27.250
Cloud Platform and
you can download

00:35:27.250 --> 00:35:30.930
that application that
agent on all your printers.

00:35:30.930 --> 00:35:32.410
It's going to be the very same.

00:35:32.410 --> 00:35:36.760
And imagine that Google
is changing its way

00:35:36.760 --> 00:35:39.790
to communicate with the
Google Cloud Platform,

00:35:39.790 --> 00:35:44.090
you just have to load the V2
of that guy and this is it.

00:35:44.090 --> 00:35:47.050
It's going to be available
for all your printer.

00:35:47.050 --> 00:35:52.000
So there is no more protocol
issues, you are future-proof.

00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:54.440
You're cost effective
because you write once.

00:35:54.440 --> 00:35:57.040
Your application, you
can deploy it everywhere

00:35:57.040 --> 00:36:04.000
in a very secure way and without
compromising the core printing

00:36:04.000 --> 00:36:07.450
knowledge of Zebra.

00:36:07.450 --> 00:36:10.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

